+A working prototype of everything below is the scratch worktree `.worktree/proto-c0116`, local branch `proto-c0116`, on c0115's prototype `proto-c0115` at 7ac8312 (c0115 rebased onto c0120's prototype at b7e75b9; c0115's design is still in review). It has two commits: a **simulation, not part of c0116** (c0115 done: its specs promoted to `tests/acceptance/c0115`, its folder in `done/`), and **the edits**, one commit. `prototype.patch` beside this document is `git diff -M proto-c0115` of that worktree, so it includes the simulation. The documents are the contract; the executor may start from the prototype. It touches none of c0120's fence and runner files (`tests/run.luau`, `tests/lib/fence.luau`, `z-nile/lib/{fence.sh,ac.sh}`, `z-nile/check.sh`).
+
+### 1. The lock, and one table for every command
+The earlier attempt (R0042 design reviews 001 to 005) computed "who holds a prepared issue" from origin `issues:` lists, which change only when a PR merges, so every round found one more command that read them stale (lesson zz-arch/lock-reads-live-state). This design reads no `issues:` list to decide a hold. Two places change at the moment membership changes, and the lock lives there:
+- **The `raft:` line.** An issue's raft is its own `raft:` line. `new raft`, `fold` and `recombine` write it in the rafting worktree, and only the rafting PR carries it to `main`. Two PRs that raft one issue edit that one line, so the second can't merge cleanly (U1: a content conflict on merge, squash and rebase; an identical edit merges cleanly, which is harmless).
+- **The raft's stamp.** The published claim stamps rafts only, with a fresh `preparing-…` value that no item on `origin/main` carries (`freshStamp`, the rule `claimStamp` already used for `active-…`). A prepared issue is never restamped. `recombine` gives its two rafts one value, and "the same claim" means "the same stamp", never "the same raft id".
+
+**The rule, once.** An issue belongs to the raft its `raft:` line names on `origin/main`. A command that claims or prepares a raft refuses an issue whose origin `raft:` names a different raft, unless that raft carries this raft's own stamp (recombine's other raft). No command restamps an issue that is `prepared` on `origin/main`. `ready` (unchanged) reads membership from `raft:` lines and refuses a raft whose `issues:` disagrees.
+
+| command | origin state it needs | what it publishes | what it writes in the worktree | refuses |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `new raft <type> <slug> <ids…>` over `new` issues (the old prep) | each id `new`, `raft:` empty | the raft (`preparing-S`) and each id (`preparing-S`), as today | each id's `raft:`, one commit | an id with `raft:` set ("already belongs to"), a type mismatch, two projects |
+| `new raft …` over prepared issues (z-raft) | each id backlog, `prepared`, `design: ready`, `raft:` empty | the raft only (`preparing-S`) | each id's `raft:`, one commit; `.cache/tracker-claim-<R>` = S | new and prepared ids together ("not both"); a `solo: true` id with others; the rest as above |
+| `fold <R> <ids…>` | R backlog `prepared` (or `preparing` with this checkout's remembered stamp: resumed, nothing published); each id prepared, `design: ready`, `raft:` empty, R's type and project | R's status line only | each id's `raft:`, R's `issues:` appended, one commit; the claim file | R new, preparing (another checkout's), active or done; a solo id with others, or into a raft holding a solo issue |
+| `recombine <K> <O> [<ids…>]` | K and O backlog, `prepared` or `new`, one project, one type; every member of both (`raft:` lines) prepared; each named id a member of O (none named: all) | K's and O's status lines, one shared fresh `preparing-S` | moved ids' `raft:` = K, K's `issues:` += them, O's `issues:` −= them, or, when O ends up empty, O's folder moved to `<root>/rafts/retired/<O>` (`git mv`) with `retired: <K>` in its document; one commit; both claim files | the two rafts the same; another project ("rafts never cross projects"), another type, active, done or preparing; a non-member of O; a solo issue with others |
+| `status <R> preparing\|new` (published raft move) | as today | R, and each origin-listed member that is **not** `prepared` (the old prep's members keep moving with their raft, so c0053's abandon stays green) | the merge of origin | a listed member whose origin `raft:` names another raft ("<id> belongs to <X> on origin/main, not <R>") |
+| `status <R> prepared` (carried, from `preparing`) | R `preparing`; each member of the local `issues:` is `prepared` with a local `design: ready` (taken as it is, never restamped), or `preparing` with R's own stamp or a claim this checkout made (`status <id> preparing` here, remembered in `.cache/tracker-claim-<id>`; stamped locally, as today) | nothing | R's (and the preparing members') `status: prepared-…` | a member another raft holds on origin, unless that raft carries R's stamp; a `preparing` member with any other stamp, another terminal's live claim (review 001 F1: "<id> is claimed by another terminal (<stamp>): drop it from <R> or release"); when some member was prepared on origin (z-raft grouped it): an empty Theme or Order & dependencies section ("<R> has no Theme: z-raft writes it before status <R> prepared"), and, with two or more members and a membership this rafting changed (a member whose origin `raft:` is not R: review 001 F5), no `design-review-NNN.md` in R's folder that is not on `origin/main` ("<R> groups <n> issues: run its coherence review (z-raft) before status <R> prepared") |
+| `release <R> [--force]` | R's origin status `preparing-S` | every backlog item whose origin status is S (git grep, `claims.carriers`): `prepared-…` when its origin `design:` is `ready`, else `new-…`; one commit | nothing: no merge (the branch is given up, and may well conflict with `main`); the claim files removed | a raft with no rafting claim; a claim this checkout does not remember, unless `--force` ("<R>'s claim S is not this checkout's: give back only your own claim (--force when the user says it is abandoned)") |
+| `status <id> preparing\|new` (an issue alone: z-design's claim) | as today | the issue's status line | the merge of origin; `preparing` remembers the claim in `.cache/tracker-claim-<id>`, `new` forgets it | `preparing` of an issue whose origin `raft:` names a raft ("<id> is in <R> on origin/main: z-raft takes it out of <R> before z-design claims it") |
+| `ready`, `stop`, `start` | unchanged | unchanged | unchanged | `ready <raftless issue>` now says "<id> has no raft: z-raft makes one (./zn raft-plan)"; `ready` still refuses a raft with a member that is not `prepared` on origin |
+
+Every new publish is preceded by `refuseStale()` (c0081's publish-guard pins it). `fold`, `recombine` and `new raft` need a clean tree, refuse the main checkout, and merge `origin/main` first, so the local tree holds everything `origin/main` does when they read membership.
+
+**The cases the earlier rounds broke, with this table** (each is a spec case, §Test plan):
+- *Concurrent rafting of one issue* (review-002 F4): both `new raft`s publish; the first PR merges; the second's `git merge origin/main` stops with `CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in <root>/issues/<id>/<id>.md`; its carried `status <R2> prepared` refuses, naming the first raft; `release R2` publishes only R2's document, and the issue's origin bytes never changed.
+- *Fold into a prepared raft* (review-005 F2): the claim publishes R's status alone; the members' origin bytes stay equal; another checkout's `fold` and `status R preparing` are refused, and so is `ready R`; the carried move takes the folded issue; after the merge `ready R` claims it.
+- *Give back a fold* (review-003 F1): `release R` puts R back to `prepared` (its design is ready on origin), with its old members; `ready R` works.
+- *Recombine* (review-005 F1): both rafts share one stamp; the carried `status K prepared` takes the moved issue whose origin `raft:` still names O, because O carries K's stamp; a raft with another stamp is refused, naming it; after the merge both rafts are readyable, and an emptied O is gone.
+- *A raft at `new` that lists a held issue* (review-005 F2's third bullet): `status <R> preparing` refuses the listed issue another raft's `raft:` names.
+- *A member re-claimed for design during the rafting* (review 001 F1): B's `status c0013 preparing` succeeds (nothing on origin says c0013 is being rafted) and merges into the rafting worktree cleanly; the carried `status RX prepared` then refuses, naming B's stamp, and leaves origin's c0013 alone; the rafting drops c0013 and prepares the rest. Once it lands, z-design's claim of a member refuses ("is in RX"). The other order (B claims after the carried move, before the landing) meets at `ready`, which refuses the raft while the member is `preparing`.
+- *Abandon* (review-004 F1): `release` (or `status R new`) never meets `prepared>new`, because prepared issues are never targets.
+
+**Who else can claim a member while a rafting is open** (review 001 F1; lesson architecture/claims-name-who-else-can-claim). The raft-only stamp leaves a member's `status:` unstamped from `new raft`/`fold`/`recombine` until the rafting PR lands. Every command that can stamp or take an issue in that window, and where it meets the rafting:
+
+| command, run by another terminal during the window | what stops it, or where the two meet |
+|---|---|
+| z-design's `status <id> preparing` | before the landing it succeeds (origin `raft:` is still empty); the rafting's carried `status <R> prepared` then refuses the member, naming the claim, because its stamp is neither R's nor this checkout's (if the carried move already ran, `ready <R>` refuses the landed raft while the member is `preparing`). After the landing it is refused: "<id> is in <R> on origin/main" |
+| `new raft … <id>` (another rafting) | publishes; the two PRs conflict on `<id>`'s `raft:` line (U1), and the later carried move refuses it ("belongs to <R>") once the first lands |
+| `fold <R2> <id>` | the same `raft:` line; `fold` into the raft being rafted is refused (it is `preparing` with a stamp this checkout does not remember) |
+| `recombine <K> <O>` | the issue is in no raft on origin (its `raft:` is empty), so it is no one's member to move; a raft being rafted is `preparing` and refused |
+| `status <R2> preparing\|new` of a raft whose stale `issues:` lists it | skips a prepared member, and refuses one whose `raft:` names another raft |
+| `release <R2>` | gives back only what carries R2's stamp; a z-raft member carries none |
+| `ready <R>`, `ready <id>` | `<R>` is `preparing`: refused; `<id>` has no raft on origin: refused |
+| `stop`, `start` | `stop` takes only active rafts; a legacy `start` needs a prepared raft |
+| `status <id> new` (another terminal giving back) | needs the issue `preparing`; a member that is `prepared` is not (`prepared>new` is not allowed) |
+
+### 2. The new commands
+Signatures in `z-nile/engine/main.luau` (each `function cmd.<name>(pos, opts)`; `raft-plan` is `cmd["raft-plan"]`):
+```
+fold <R-id> <issue>...                        put prepared, raftless issues into a prepared backlog raft (z-raft)
+recombine <keeper> <other> [<issue>...]       move the other backlog raft's members (all, or those named) into the
+                                              keeper; retire the other when it ends up empty (z-raft)
+release <R-id> [--force]                      give back a rafting claim: what carries its stamp goes back to
+                                              prepared (design ready) or new (z-raft)
+raft-plan [--apply]                           print z-raft's plan for every prepared, raftless issue (--apply runs it)
+promote-check <R-id|issue-id>                 (was <R-id>)
+```
