 ### Scalability
+- `raft-plan` reads every item document once (the same reads as `list`) and runs `touchedModules` per issue; the plan is O(n²) over candidate features of one project and type (pairs of Touched lists), which is tens of issues: well under a second (the CLI spec's fixture plans in ~0.3 s inside a 4 s case).
+- `release` runs one `git grep -l` over the project roots on `origin/main` (as `claimStamp` does) and one publish.
+- `fold` and `recombine` merge `origin/main` before and after their one publish; `recombine` reads the members locally (after the merge), never every document on origin.
+- `audit`'s orphan check reads each issue document's frontmatter once more (the gate's audit stays under 0.2 s on the real tree).
+
 ### Maintainability
+- One pure module holds the whole no-argument policy (`raftplan.luau`), with one function per rule input, so a new rule is a row in its spec. One overlap rule for `workstreams` and the plan.
+- One rule for every claim (§1), stated once in zz-rules' **Rafting** bullet and once in this table, and one give-back verb.
+- Messages reuse the existing refusal shapes; no new file format: the coherence review is a `design-review-NNN.md`, so `lessons` and `metrics` read it with their existing readers.
+
 ### Usability
+- z-design's next step is `/z-raft`; z-raft's is `/z-ready <R-id>`; `ready` on a raftless issue says "z-raft makes one (./zn raft-plan)".
+- `raft-plan` prints copy-pasteable commands, so the plan is reviewable before `--apply`.
+- Every refusal names the item and the rule (the raft that holds an issue, the stamp that isn't yours, the missing Theme).
 
