+## Guesses (pending user)
+1. G1: `fold <R> <ids…>` is a new command for adding raftless prepared issues to a raft (the Problem's "folds … into one open prepared … raft" needs one; recombine moves between rafts only).
+2. G2: `release <R> [--force]` is the published give-back of a rafting claim, and it refuses a claim this checkout doesn't remember unless `--force` (the programme review's F4; zz-rules' "ownership is documented, not checked" gains this one checked exception).
+3. G3: the solo rule is enforced by the commands too (`new raft`, `fold`, `recombine` refuse a solo issue with others), so "z-raft never folds a solo issue with others" holds with an argument as well; to raft a solo issue with others, the user edits its `solo:` (a design decision).
+4. G4: `size:` is the designer's estimate with no formula (U3 failed), calibrated afterwards by metrics' size table; an open raft with a member that has no `size:` is not open to the no-argument plan (review 001 F3), so today's open rafts take new members only with an argument.
+5. G5: a raft of one issue gets `single: true` only when the issue is solo or over the cap; any other lone issue's raft is `single: false`, so later issues of its type can fold in. Existing `single: true` rafts are never folded into.
+6. G6: z-raft's coherence review is exactly one round; a finding that needs a member's design to change takes the member out of the raft (back to z-design) instead of a second round.
+7. G7: the coherence review applies to every raft of two or more issues (the decision's "several issues"), defects and chores included, not only features; it is enforced by the carried move (a `design-review-NNN.md` in the raft's folder that isn't on `origin/main`).
+8. G8: a released `new raft` is left on `origin/main` as an orphan (publish never deletes), warned about by `audit`, and retired by a later `recombine` into any raft of its project and type, in that rafting PR.
+9. G9: z-design designs exactly one issue per run; features that share a surface are designed apart and meet in z-raft's coherence review (the decided option, not "design several together").
+10. G10: rafting worktrees are named `raft-<slug>`, and rafting PRs merge under the same rule as preps ("ask the user before merging a rafting PR they didn't ask for"); c0117 decides whether an unattended one may merge.
+11. G11: `raft-plan` reads the local tree (run after `./zn sync`), not `origin/main` directly; `--apply` runs in-process and stops at the first refusal, leaving the claims made so far for resume or `release`.
+12. G12: c0116 itself is `solo: true` (its "sequenced rafts" decision) and `size: 1200` (the prototype's measured code and docs lines, below).
